00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:15.100
This is one block in one city in one state
of the United States.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:20.499
The US Constitution’s Article 1, Section
2 prescribes something that must happen on

00:00:20.499 --> 00:00:25.239
this block: “Representatives and direct
Taxes shall be apportioned among the several

00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:30.339
States which may be included within this Union,
according to their respective Numbers…

00:00:30.339 --> 00:00:34.120
The actual Enumeration shall be made within
three Years after the first Meeting of the

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.730
Congress of the United States, and within
every subsequent Term of ten Years, in such

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.870
Manner as they shall by Law direct.”

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.620
What that means is that every ten years, the
US government must determine how many people

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.940
live on this block, in addition to how many
people live on every other block in every

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.510
other city in every other state and territory
of the United States.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:59.130
They must determine the population of the
country—a straightforward task, but one

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:04.769
that thousands of US Census Bureau employees
spend a decade preparing for, and a year and

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.330
$15.6 billion executing.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:12.640
It turns out that counting more than 300 million
people isn’t exactly easy.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.660
Instead of looking at how the Census counts
every one of those hundreds of millions, lets

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:23.420
rather look at how they count the couple dozen
people living on this one city block.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.920
The Bureau started preparing for the 2020
Census on November 1st, 2011.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.080
They have a staff of about 4,000 who plan
and coordinate each Census, along the their

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.190
other programs, and then, every ten years,
when it comes time to actually conduct the

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:42.479
Census, they employ more than half a million
other individuals temporarily.

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:46.850
The 4,000 full-time employees, though, spend
much of the decade forming their operational

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.680
plan, the first draft of which was done in
late 2015.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.100
They then spent the next three years refining
that.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.580
A significant element of this plan includes
deciding where the Census’ field offices

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.590
will be located.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.920
You see, the Census divides the country up
into six regions which are headquartered with

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.990
permanent offices in Los Angeles, Denver,
Chicago, Atlanta, Philadelphia, and New York

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:08.990
City.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:14.400
Then, within those regions, the Census opens
up 248 temporary field offices to conduct

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.170
local operations.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.270
These field offices are distributed based
on density so Colorado, for example, only

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.810
has four while New York, on the other hand,
has 21.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:28.400
Each of these offices has to hire hundreds
or thousands of enumerators to go out into

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.870
the streets and conduct the Census.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:35.280
Finding half a million temporary workers is,
of course, incredibly difficult and it’s

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.850
even more difficult for the 2020 census given
that the country currently has the lowest

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.140
unemployment rate in recent history.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.260
In order to attract those half million people
they do have to account for the drastically

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.500
different wage conditions across the country.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.739
Enumerators in Mesa County, Colorado, where
our block is located, for example, are paid

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:58.751
$16.00 per hour which, for this area, is a
pretty decent hourly wage—especially for

00:02:58.751 --> 00:03:01.730
a low-skilled job requiring few qualifications.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:05.230
Of course, if you offered that pay in New
York City, on the other hand, it would be

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:11.690
far less attractive so the pay there is upped
to between $25.00 and $28.00 an hour.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:16.060
Despite the decent pay, though, the Census
still always has enormous difficulty filling

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:20.610
all their positions so they spend years teeing
up all these workers.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.310
The first of the temporary hires for the 2020
Census happened in mid-2018.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:29.670
That’s because, before the Census can go
and ask every household on this block how

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:34.310
many people live there, they have to figure
out where each of these households is.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:38.480
In the past ten years, new houses could have
been built, structures could have been subdivided,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.780
and plenty more could have changed so this
is a crucial step for accuracy.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.709
In addition, if a homeless person is living
in this alley, if someone’s living in a

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:51.640
motorhome in someone’s driveway, or if there
are any other abnormal residences, this too

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.270
needs to be counted.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:57.690
Previously, the Census would have sent someone
to walk this block, in addition to every other

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.519
block in the country, and mark down where
each residence is, which clearly took an enormous

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:04.340
amount of person-hours.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:09.159
With the 2020 Census, though, they’re shifting
technique to use more and more satellite imagery

00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:11.050
to identify residences.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.750
Of course, that’s not perfect.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:18.580
For example, it’s hard to tell if this is
one residence or two, so for any areas that

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:22.370
they’re not sure about they’ll still send
people to check in person.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:26.669
At the conclusion of this process, at least
in theory, they should have a list of every

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:30.040
single place where people live in the US.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.780
Also in 2018, the Census Bureau had to submit
to Congress the questions they planned to

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.560
ask the American public.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.510
The Census is incredibly simple—after all,
it has to be if they expect everyone to answer

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:42.510
it.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:46.490
It asks four background questions, five about
the primary resident, then seven about every

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.580
other resident of a given household.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:54.450
Despite this, the Bureau spends an enormous
amount of time debating how to ask these questions.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:59.470
Question selection is so complex that they
published a 98-page document explaining how

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:02.550
they selected the 2020 questions and their
wording.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:07.470
For example, in question three, for the non-primary
residents, they added specification on whether

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:11.800
their relation to the primary resident was
as a same-sex or opposite-sex couple.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:16.320
In the past there was the option to mark the
relation as a spouse or unmarried partner,

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.060
but there was no distinction between same-sex
or opposite-sex.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:24.910
They did, however, calculate what percentage
of couples were same-sex based on how they

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:27.880
responded to the question asking if they were
male or female.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.660
There was an issue with that, though.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:34.490
Say there’s a married couple living in this
house consisting of one man and one woman.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:38.460
If the woman accidentally marks her sex as
male and also marked her relationship as a

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.639
spouse, they would be counted as a same-sex
married couple.

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:47.729
Given that the overall proportion of Census-surveyed
couples who are same-sex is small, these mistakes

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:50.139
could significantly skew the data.

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:54.610
With the redesign of this question, it acts
as a failsafe so that even if the woman in

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:59.380
this house marks her sex as male, they’ll
still be counted as an opposite-sex couple.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:03.229
These are the tiny details the Bureau has
to think about for each and every question

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:04.289
and change.

00:06:04.289 --> 00:06:09.710
They have to think about how to best structure
them to get the best possible accuracy.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:14.150
Once they have their questions, one of the
final things the Census needs to figure out

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:17.560
is how they’re actually going to ask for
responses.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:22.880
Our block is located in Grand Junction, Colorado,
which is a small city, so it, along with most

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.770
of the country, simply receives a mailed invitation
instructing the recipient to complete the

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.160
census online.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:33.740
This works well for most of this relatively
urban area, but not for all of it.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.940
For example, residences in this small section
of Grand Junction will receive both an invitation

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.710
to complete the Census online and a paper
Census form.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:46.650
That’s because it’s home to a number of
assisted living facilities and therefore a

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:51.669
number of older individuals who are far less
likely to respond online.

00:06:51.669 --> 00:06:55.919
About 22% of the country is classified by
the Census as areas where individuals are

00:06:55.919 --> 00:07:00.940
less likely to respond online and therefore
receive paper Census forms as well.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.770
There are, though, areas where both of those
options won’t work.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:10.160
Nearby Eagle County, for example, has a population
where one-third are of hispanic origin and

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.860
plenty of that population has limited or no
English-language skills.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:20.039
Therefore, residences here receive a bilingual
invitation to complete the Census online.

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:23.970
There’s then one more category, used in
areas like Costilla county where there is

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:29.319
a large Spanish population and limited internet
access, where bilingual paper Census forms

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:30.419
are sent out.

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:34.850
The Census classifies the entirety of the
country into one of these four categories

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:38.230
in order to have the best possible chance
of getting an answer.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:43.330
Once offices are opened, employees are hired,
residences are logged, questions are written,

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:47.800
and letters are planned, it’s finally time
for the Census to start in earnest.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.819
The first Census responses cannot happen until
2020 starts, and all responses must be in

00:07:52.819 --> 00:07:55.210
by the time 2020 finishes.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:08:00.970
They have exactly 366 days to count every
single person in the US, and deliver that

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.930
count to the US Congress.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:07.800
Our block in Grand Junction is far from the
first place to be counted though.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.990
That happens in Toksook Bay, Alaska.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:13.690
Census workers arrive there in late-January,
2020.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:20.099
Toksook Bay, along with 200 or so other rural
Alaskan towns, are exceptions in the overall

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:25.530
timeline of the Census count as, in mid-Winter,
when the water and ground are frozen, one

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:30.870
can get around relatively easily on snowmobile
whereas, later on, when most of the country’s

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:36.039
Census counting is done, it’s very tough
to travel when the ice and snow are melting.

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:41.949
Down in the lower 48, the seasons are far
less of a concern so in mid-March, 95% of

00:08:41.949 --> 00:08:47.089
American households receive a mailed invitation
to fill in the Census online.

00:08:47.089 --> 00:08:51.449
That includes our block, but if the earlier
investigation found someone living in a mobile

00:08:51.449 --> 00:08:56.629
home in someone’s driveway there, they would
likely get their invitation hand-delivered.

00:08:56.629 --> 00:09:00.689
Mobile homes, residences in areas with recent
natural disasters, or households that use

00:09:00.689 --> 00:09:05.100
PO boxes don’t get mail delivered to their
door so that’s when the Census uses this

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:09.100
hand-delivery technique, which is used in
about 4% of cases.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:14.959
A small other proportion—less than 1%—is
visited and surveyed in person from the first

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:19.350
instance, mostly in the most remote areas
of Alaska and Maine, in addition to on some

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.589
American Indian reservations.

00:09:21.589 --> 00:09:26.189
On our block, like most others, if households
don’t respond to the first invitation, they’ll

00:09:26.189 --> 00:09:31.269
get a reminder letter, then a reminder postcard,
then a reminder letter with a paper questionnaire,

00:09:31.269 --> 00:09:34.889
then one more reminder postcard before the
Census gives up on the household responding

00:09:34.889 --> 00:09:37.050
by internet, mail, or phone.

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:41.829
Typically, about a third of the houses on
this block will not have responded on time,

00:09:41.829 --> 00:09:45.249
even though it’s a legal requirement.

00:09:45.249 --> 00:09:49.410
Non-respondent residences on our block will
get a visit from an enumerator, but they might

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.319
not be the first to get visited.

00:09:52.319 --> 00:09:54.379
In-person visits are prioritized strategically.

00:09:54.379 --> 00:09:58.639
For example, knowing that college students
will start move out from their dorms and student

00:09:58.639 --> 00:10:04.660
housing starting in mid-May, they send enumerators
to non-respondents at college campuses first.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:08.800
Each of our houses will get three visits,
then if they don’t respond on the fourth,

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:12.610
they’ll also try to ask neighbors if they
know the details of the residents of the given

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:13.610
household.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:17.420
They’ll try both the household and the neighbors
again for the fifth and sixth try, but then,

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:21.839
if the household doesn’t respond after five
mailings, six visits, and three knocks on

00:10:21.839 --> 00:10:27.730
the neighbor’s doors, then the Census finally
decides that the residence must be uninhabited.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.790
Of course, in addition to counting everyone
in every household, they also have to count

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:34.449
people who don’t live in a household.

00:10:34.449 --> 00:10:39.129
For example, to count homeless people, the
Census will set up at shelters, soup kitchens,

00:10:39.129 --> 00:10:42.779
food vans, in addition to just going out into
the streets.

00:10:42.779 --> 00:10:46.929
Another challenge is counting people who live
and work on American ships who don’t have

00:10:46.929 --> 00:10:48.529
a permanent residence.

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:53.139
The Census runs a special program for this—the
Maritime and Military Vessel Enumeration—where

00:10:53.139 --> 00:10:57.540
ships are sent Census kits including customized
forms with different questions targeted at

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:58.560
seafarers.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:02.170
These are all then sent back to the Census
to be included in the count, and a variety

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:06.040
of techniques are used to assure that nobody
is double-counted.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:10.610
Throughout the second half of 2020, in-person
visits will finish up, special operations

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:14.990
will conclude, and the Census will get into
a huge number crunching operation to figure

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:19.259
out, with as much accuracy as possible, what
the final count is.

00:11:19.259 --> 00:11:23.809
They not only have to give that total number,
but they also have to break it down by state,

00:11:23.809 --> 00:11:29.959
by county, by town, by race, by age, by relationship
status, and by much, much more.

00:11:29.959 --> 00:11:37.209
It takes some time, but there’s a very,
very specific deadline—December 31st, 2020.

00:11:37.209 --> 00:11:41.269
The US Congress needs to know the count by
then in order to re-allocate seats in the

00:11:41.269 --> 00:11:48.100
House of Representatives proportionally to
the population of the states.

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:53.449
In the 2010 Census, 308,745,538 people were
counted as living in the US.

00:11:53.449 --> 00:11:59.730
In 2020, the number is expected to be around
330 million but, to know for sure, we’ll

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:04.829
just have to wait for December 31st, 2020.

00:12:04.829 --> 00:12:09.509
One of the big jobs at the Census Bureau is
as a math statistician and for that, according

00:12:09.509 --> 00:12:13.879
to their job listing, you need to be adept
at things like number theory, statistics,

00:12:13.879 --> 00:12:19.689
probability, algebra, and calculus, all of
which you can learn or brush up on with Brilliant.

00:12:19.689 --> 00:12:23.889
Each of their courses for each of those subjects
is interactive, wonderfully designed, and

00:12:23.889 --> 00:12:28.470
helps you learn complex topics like calculus
not through rote memorization, but rather

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:32.579
through breaking down the concepts into small,
intuitive chunks and then building it all

00:12:32.579 --> 00:12:34.399
back together.

00:12:34.399 --> 00:12:38.820
This is how you can truly understand calculus
or number theory or statistics, rather than

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.540
just learning the process to solve problems.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:45.449
If math isn’t your thing, they also have
dozens of amazing other courses on subjects

00:12:45.449 --> 00:12:50.050
like scientific thinking, quantum mechanics,
computer science, and plenty more.

